Chapter: LirCheck
  cites Foreword chapter MathLib

 Drives the REAL LIR lowering (IR/Lir.codex, via the `lir-dump` pass) over
 real lowered IR. Each straight-line, `if`, and scalar `match` function
 below is lowered to the flat LIR, structurally verified, and dumped; the
 non-lowerable ones (a call, an act block, a constructor-pattern match) are
 declined. There is no reimplementation here: the shapes are ordinary
 Codex, the compiler lowers them to IRExpr, and `-Passes lir-dump` runs
 `lower-def-to-lir` + `lir-check-func` over each and prints one line per
 definition to the compile log. The pinned answers live in
 `lir-check.lir-expected`, read off the real lowering and hand-checked.

 The default battery compiles this file with the DEFAULT pipeline (the LIR
 pass does not run) and checks the runtime output against
 `lir-check.expected` -- that pins that the functions actually COMPUTE
 correctly, so the LIR dump is being read against working programs.

 Each lowerable function also gets a `LIVE <name>: bN in {..}` line beside
 its `LIR` line, pinning the backward liveness (Section: Liveness in
 `Lir.codex`): a parameter is live at the entry block, the result vreg is
 live at the exit block, and a value is live across exactly the blocks
 between its definition and its last use.

 A third `ALLOC <name>: v0->r0 v1->r0 ... [alloc-check: ok]` line pins the
 linear-scan register assignment (Section: Allocator) and the allocation
 verifier's verdict on it (Section: Allocation Verifier). The assignment
 now shows register reuse -- two vregs with disjoint live ranges share a
 register (e.g. sl-mix's v2 reuses v0's) -- and sl-wide, with five live
 parameters over four registers, shows spill slots (`sN`). The verifier
 runs over the real allocation and accepts it; corrupting the assignment
 makes it fire.

 The shapes:

  - sl-add:     two params, one add          -> one block
  - sl-mix:     a let feeding an add          -> one block
  - sl-nest:    nested binary over 2 params   -> one block
  - sl-wide:    five params summed            -> spills: 4 regs, 5 live params
  - if-min:     an if with literal arms       -> four blocks, fused compare
  - if-operand: an if feeding an add          -> the join block consumes the if
  - if-nest:    an if nested in an arm        -> six blocks, non-contiguous order
  - sw-min:     match, one literal + wildcard -> decision chain, one test
  - sw-three:   match, two literals + wildcard -> chained tests
  - sw-var:     match, literal + var catch-all -> the var binds the scrutinee
  - sw-ctor:    match on constructor patterns -> tag load, then field load per bind
  - nl-call:    a call the leaf inliner eats -> lowers as a plain add
  - if-even:    a power-of-two remainder against zero -> emits ONE `test`
  - if-odd:     the same fused, negated                -> `test`, inverted branch
  - if-mod4:    a wider power of two                   -> `test` against mask 3
  - if-mod3:    a remainder by three                   -> NOT fused (not a power of two)
  - if-mod-one: a remainder against one, not zero      -> NOT fused (not against zero)

 **These five are pinned by their VALUES first, and by their dump lines as
 well since 2026-07-19.** They were answers-only for a reason worth keeping
 written down: `build/lir-dump-test.ps1` ran `-Passes lir-dump`, and `-Passes`
 REPLACES the pipeline rather than adding to it, so the recording ran with
 `fold-constants` and `inline-leaf-calls` switched off. All five dumped as
 `call math-mod` with an ordinary compare while every one of them emitted
 something else, and this prose concluded the inliner "runs later, in the
 INLINE phase". It does not. It runs in the pass pipeline the flag was
 silently emptying.

 The harness now names the default passes alongside `lir-dump`, so the pin
 records the program that actually ships: `if-even`, `if-odd` and `if-mod4`
 dump as a single `test`, and -- this is the part that was unreachable before
 -- `if-mod3` dumps as `rem` and `if-mod-one` keeps its `cmp v1 #1`, so both
 guards on the fusion are now visible in the pin rather than resting on the
 answers alone.

 The last two are the negative half and they are the point of the pair. The
 fusion is guarded on a power-of-two divisor and on a comparison against
 zero, and a guard that nothing tests is a guard that is not there. Both are
 chosen so a broken guard changes an answer rather than merely a shape:
 `if-mod3 6` is 1, and a mask-2 fusion escaping onto it would say 0.

 `if-even` is called with negative arguments on purpose. The mask and the
 remainder do not agree in general -- they differ in sign and value for a
 negative dividend, because the remainder follows the quotient's rounding and
 the mask does not -- and they agree on zero-ness alone, which is exactly and
 only what the fusion asks. -4 is even and -5 is odd, and the fused form has
 to say so.

Section: Shapes

  sl-add : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sl-add (x) (y) = x + y

  sl-mix : Integer -> Integer
  sl-mix (x) = let a = x * 2 in a + 3

  sl-nest : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sl-nest (x) (y) = (x + y) * (x - y)

  sl-wide : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sl-wide (a) (b) (c) (d) (e) = a + b + c + d + e

  if-min : Integer -> Integer
  if-min (n) = if n > 0 then 1 else 0

  if-operand : Integer -> Integer
  if-operand (n) = (if n > 0 then 10 else 20) + n

  if-nest : Integer -> Integer
  if-nest (n) = if n > 0 then (if n > 5 then 2 else 1) else 0

  sw-min : Integer -> Integer
  sw-min (n) =
    when n
      is 0 -> 10
      is otherwise -> 20

  sw-three : Integer -> Integer
  sw-three (n) =
    when n
      is 0 -> 100
      is 1 -> 200
      is otherwise -> 300

  sw-var : Integer -> Integer
  sw-var (n) =
    when n
      is 0 -> 0
      is x -> x + 1

  sw-or : Integer -> Integer
  sw-or (n) =
    when n
      is 0 | 1 -> 5
      is otherwise -> 9

  Sw =
   | SwA (Integer)
   | SwB

  sw-ctor : Sw -> Integer
  sw-ctor (s) =
    when s
      is SwA (x) -> x
      is SwB -> 0

  nl-call : Integer -> Integer
  nl-call (n) = sl-add n n

 `col-hue` from the foreword's Color chapter, kept whole because every part of
 it is load-bearing. Five parameters, so the allocator runs out of the
 argument registers it prefers; and a division, which masks RDX out of the
 free set for everything live across it, so parameter 2 cannot keep the
 register it arrived in and is placed in RCX -- where parameter 3 arrives.
 Emitted in parameter order, `mov rcx,rdx` then `mov rbx,rcx` hands `mx` the
 value of `b`.

 That returned 60 instead of 0: `rgb-to-hsl` called red yellow, and
 `color-test` had been failing on the selector for as long as the leaf
 register file has existed. Drop the division and it does not reproduce.
 Drop a parameter and it does not reproduce. This is the shape.

 Pinned by its ANSWERS. No benchmark can see this -- it is a wrong answer,
 not a slow one -- and no verifier can either: the entry moves are emitted
 before the blocks and are not LIR instructions, so `[alloc-check: ok]` below
 is a verdict on a function whose prologue the verifier never looked at.

  pmv-clash : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pmv-clash (a) (b) (c) (d) (e) =
    if d == a then let raw = (b - c) * 60 / e
      in if raw < 0 then raw + 360 else raw
    else if d == b then (c - a) * 60 / e + 120
    else (a - b) * 60 / e + 240

 The function result is a use. `s` is both the result and the source of the
 one join move the then-arm makes, and the else-arm's division keeps the dead
 let alive through simplification. A coalescer that counts only instruction
 operands sees a single use, folds the add into the join vreg, and the result
 reads a vreg nothing defines -- RAX by default, so the else path answered
 the division: coal-result 5 -2 said -50 where it must say 6. The then path
 answers 6 either way, which is why the shape survives a spot check.

  coal-result : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  coal-result (a) (b) = let s = a + 1 in let y = (if b > 0 then s else 100 / b) in s

  if-even : Integer -> Integer
  if-even (n) = if math-mod n 2 == 0 then 1 else 0

  if-odd : Integer -> Integer
  if-odd (n) = if math-mod n 2 /= 0 then 1 else 0

  if-mod4 : Integer -> Integer
  if-mod4 (n) = if math-mod n 4 == 0 then 1 else 0

  if-mod3 : Integer -> Integer
  if-mod3 (n) = if math-mod n 3 == 0 then 1 else 0

  if-mod-one : Integer -> Integer
  if-mod-one (n) = if math-mod n 2 == 1 then 1 else 0

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni (show (sl-add 3 4))
   print-line-uni (show (sl-mix 5))
   print-line-uni (show (sl-nest 5 3))
   print-line-uni (show (sl-wide 1 2 3 4 5))
   print-line-uni (show (if-min 4))
   print-line-uni (show (if-operand 4))
   print-line-uni (show (if-nest 8))
   print-line-uni (show (sw-min 0))
   print-line-uni (show (sw-three 1))
   print-line-uni (show (sw-var 7))
   print-line-uni (show (sw-or 1))
   print-line-uni (show (sw-ctor (SwA 42)))
   print-line-uni (show (sw-ctor SwB))
   print-line-uni (show (if-even 4))
   print-line-uni (show (if-even 7))
   print-line-uni (show (if-even (-4)))
   print-line-uni (show (if-even (-5)))
   print-line-uni (show (if-odd 7))
   print-line-uni (show (if-odd (-5)))
   print-line-uni (show (if-mod4 8))
   print-line-uni (show (if-mod4 6))
   print-line-uni (show (if-mod3 6))
   print-line-uni (show (if-mod3 7))
   print-line-uni (show (if-mod-one 7))
   print-line-uni (show (pmv-clash 255 0 0 255 255))
   print-line-uni (show (pmv-clash 0 255 0 255 255))
   print-line-uni (show (pmv-clash 0 0 255 255 255))
   print-line-uni (show (coal-result 5 (-2)))
   print-line-uni (show (coal-result 5 3))
  end
